   $q$Read the sentences: "The trail was closed. Heavy rain had washed out the lower bridge." Which choice combines them to show the correct cause and effect?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The trail was closed. Because heavy rain had washed out the lower bridge.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subordinate-clause-fragment"},{"text":"The trail was closed because heavy rain had washed out the lower bridge.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The trail was closed, heavy rain had washed out the lower bridge.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-created"},{"text":"Because the trail was closed, heavy rain had washed out the lower bridge.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The trail was closed because heavy rain had washed out the lower bridge.$q$, $q$"Because" correctly introduces the cause, the washed-out bridge, and attaches it to the main clause. Putting "because" before "the trail was closed" makes the closing seem to cause the rain damage, which reverses the logic.$q$, 'draft', null)
