  ('0485e918-249c-5bc8-98dd-2e9d18107aa0', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-sentence-structure'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentences: "Researchers released the tagged sea turtles at dawn. Tracking each one by satellite for the next eight months and posting the routes online for students." Which revision fixes the error?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Researchers released the tagged sea turtles at dawn and tracked each one by satellite for the next eight months, posting the routes online for students.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Researchers released the tagged sea turtles at dawn, they tracked each one by satellite for the next eight months and posted the routes online for students.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-created"},{"text":"Researchers released the tagged sea turtles at dawn, tracking each one by satellite for the next eight months, they posted the routes online for students.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-created"},{"text":"Researchers released the tagged sea turtles at dawn; tracking each one by satellite for the next eight months and posting the routes online for students.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-before-phrase"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Researchers released the tagged sea turtles at dawn and tracked each one by satellite for the next eight months, posting the routes online for students.$q$, $q$Giving "Researchers" a second verb, "tracked," and attaching "posting the routes online" as a phrase turns the fragment into part of one complete sentence. The version with "they tracked" after a comma joins two independent clauses with only a comma, which is a comma splice.$q$, 'draft', null)
